    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="1" build="19">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.1b19</English>
                <French>v15.1b19</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="11">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.11</English>
                <French>v1.11</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="2">
                    <English>Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="5" coolingSeasonMonths="4" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="false" isLightingEnergySaver="false" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="12.2" heatingNighttime="18.9" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, your house does not have a lot of excess air leakage, however I did notice some air leakage around the new window in your stairwell. I recommend air sealing around the frame before you add the trim.



Air leakage was also observed around some of the basement windows and your attic hatch. I recommend you consider air sealing work in these areas.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $600&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

&lt;b&gt;Wall Insulation:&lt;/b&gt;



The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, with an additional 0.75 inches of rigid insulation that was added during your siding upgrade for a total of approximately R15. I have modelled the savings for an additional R15 on your exterior walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing the R value in your walls by R15, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. If you also insulate the wall between your kitchen and your porch, you could bring your wall insulation rebate up to a total of $1,875.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

&lt;b&gt;Ceilings (Gable):&lt;/b&gt;



The attic of your house was assumed to have 6 inches (R20) vermiculite insulation. I have modelled adding 9 inches (R30) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 9 inches (R30) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $185&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$375 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

&lt;b&gt;Basements:&lt;/b&gt;



Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with R12 insulation, and the pony walls are 2x8 studs with R28 with 0.75 inches of rigid insulation on the outside. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15)  rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of the pony walls, similar to the main wall upgrade. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$330 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



</foundation>
                <windows>

&lt;b&gt;Windows:&lt;/b&gt;



As all of your basement windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



I have upgraded these windows as part of my upgrade recommendations as there are two sliding windows in the basement, and I noticed drafts around some of these windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 5 basement windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Domestic Hot Water:&lt;/b&gt;



The upgrade recommendation for your domestic hot water is integrated into the boiler upgrade recommendation.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Oil Boiler:&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency, direct vent, condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency propane furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether. This will also make your hot water tank more efficient.



At your request, I have also modelled the addition of a pellet boiler to your system. If this is something you are interested in I strongly recommend speaking to an HVAC specialist before making any decisions, and to make sure a new pellet boiler is sized correctly.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a direct vent, condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $615&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;46.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. You will also increase the energy efficiency of your domestic hot water heater. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a pellet boiler, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $575&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program. However, this would potentially increase your annual energy usage.



To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you should apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year for pellet boilers. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

&lt;b&gt;Solar Upgrade:&lt;/b&gt;



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $805&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

&lt;b&gt;Water Conservation:&lt;/b&gt;



I recommend you replace the toilet in your basement. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
